answer:
### What’s happening?

* **`map.loadImage` changed in MapLibre v4** – it no longer accepts a callback.  
  It now **returns a `Promise`** that resolves with `{ data: ImageBitmap }`.  
  Because you’re still passing a callback, the promise is created but the
  callback is ignored, so the image never gets added and any markers that
  rely on that image appear as a missing-icon (the default “pin” is just a
  transparent square).

* **Missing marker icons** are a direct symptom of the same problem: the
  image you tried to register (`'pin'`) never made it into the style’s
  sprite, so the layer can’t find it.

Below is a quick checklist and the exact code you need to get it working
again, plus a few extra things to watch for after a Mapbox → MapLibre
migration.

---

## 1️⃣ Convert `loadImage` to a promise (or `async/await`)

```js
// ❌ Old Mapbox style – does NOT work in MapLibre ≥ v4
// map.loadImage(url, (err, img) => { … });

/* ✅ New MapLibre style – use the returned promise */
function addPinImage(map, url) {
  // You can use async/await
  return map.loadImage(url)
    .then(({ data }) => {
      // `data` is an ImageBitmap (or HTMLImageElement in older browsers)
      map.addImage('pin', data);
    })
    .catch(err => {
      console.error('Failed to load pin image:', err);
      // Decide how you want to handle the failure (fallback image, etc.)
    });
}

/* Or with async/await */
async function addPinImageAsync(map, url) {
  try {
    const { data } = await map.loadImage(url);
    map.addImage('pin', data);
  } catch (err) {
    console.error('Failed to load pin image:', err);
  }
}

/* Example usage – after the map is ready */
map.on('load', () => {
  addPinImage(map, 'https://example.com/images/pin.png')
    .then(() => {
      // Now you can add a layer that uses the image
      map.addLayer({
        id: 'my-pins',
        type: 'symbol',
        source: 'my-geojson',
        layout: {
          'icon-image': 'pin',
          'icon-size': 0.8
        }
      });
    });
});
```

> **Why the change?**  
> Starting with v4.0.0 MapLibre unified its async API (e.g. `getCluster…`,
> `setRTLTextPlugin`, etc.) to return promises, making it easier to use
> `await` and to compose calls. The old callback signature was removed.

---

## 2️⃣ Verify the **style sprite** (if you’re using a style that expects a
sprite)

When you migrated you probably kept a Mapbox style URL such as
`mapbox://styles/...`. Even if you replaced it with a non-Mapbox style,
the style JSON may still reference a **Mapbox sprite**:

```json
{
  "sprite": "mapbox://sprites/mapbox/streets-v12"
}
```

MapLibre cannot resolve `mapbox://` URLs, so any `icon-image` that points
to a sprite entry will be missing. You have two options:

| Option | What to do |
|--------|------------|
| **Replace the whole style** with a ready-made open-source style (e.g. OpenFreeMap, OSM Bright, MapTiler Basic). These styles already contain a working `sprite` URL that points to a public PNG/JSON pair. | `style: 'https://tiles.openfreemap.org/styles/liberty'` |
| **Keep your custom style** but **swap out the sprite URLs**. Edit the style JSON (or use Maputnik) and change `"sprite": "mapbox://…"` to a URL that serves a PNG + JSON sprite you host yourself (or a public one from MapTiler, etc.). | `sprite: 'https://my.cdn.com/sprites/my-style'` |

If you’re only adding a single custom image with `addImage`, you can **ignore the sprite** entirely – just make sure the image is added **before** any layer that references it (as shown in the code above).

---

## 3️⃣ Double-check the **marker / symbol layer**

If you’re using a `symbol` layer that references `'pin'`, make sure:

1. The image is added **once** (calling `addImage` again with the same name will throw).
2. The layer’s `layout['icon-image']` exactly matches the name you used in `addImage`.
3. The layer is added **after** the image (or you call `map.addLayer` inside the same `then`/`await` block).

```js
// Correct order
await map.loadImage(pinUrl);
map.addImage('pin', data);
map.addLayer({
  id: 'my-pins',
  type: 'symbol',
  source: 'my-geojson',
  layout: {
    'icon-image': 'pin',
    'icon-allow-overlap': true
  }
});
```

---

## 4️⃣ Quick “cheat-sheet” for the most common migration breakages

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| `map.loadImage` callback never runs | Using MapLibre ≥ v4 (callback removed) | Use the promise (`await map.loadImage(...)`) |
| Marker icons appear as a blank square | Image never added (see above) **or** style’s `sprite` still points to a `mapbox://` URL | Add the image via `loadImage` **or** replace the sprite URL |
| Console shows `Error: Image "pin" already exists` | You call `addImage('pin', …)` more than once (e.g. on every `load` event) | Guard with `if (!map.hasImage('pin')) { … }` or move the call to a one-time init block |
| `map.addImage` throws `Error: Image data must be an ImageBitmap, HTMLImageElement, HTMLCanvasElement, or HTMLVideoElement` | You passed the wrong value (e.g. the whole response object) | Use the `data` property from the promise result (`{ data }`) |
| Missing glyphs / labels | Style’s `glyphs` URL still uses `mapbox://fonts/...` | Replace with a public glyphs URL (e.g. `https://fonts.openmaptiles.org/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf`) or host your own. See the **maplibre-fonts-glyphs** skill for details. |

---

## 5️⃣ Full minimal example (ES-module build)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – custom pin</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6.0.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script type="module">
  import * as maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6.0.0/dist/maplibre-gl.mjs';

  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://tiles.openfreemap.org/styles/liberty', // any non-Mapbox style
    center: [-122.42, 37.78],
    zoom: 12
  });

  // Example GeoJSON source
  const geojson = {
    type: 'FeatureCollection',
    features: [{
      type: 'Feature',
      geometry: { type: 'Point', coordinates: [-122.42, 37.78] },
      properties: {}
    }]
  };

  map.on('load', async () => {
    // 1️⃣ Add the source
    map.addSource('my-geojson', { type: 'geojson', data: geojson });

    // 2️⃣ Load and register the custom pin image
    try {
      const { data } = await map.loadImage('https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mapbox/mapbox-gl-js/main/docs/assets/custom_marker.png');
      map.addImage('pin', data);
    } catch (e) {
      console.error('Could not load pin image', e);
      return;
    }

    // 3️⃣ Add a symbol layer that uses the image
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'my-pins',
      type: 'symbol',
      source: 'my-geojson',
      layout: {
        'icon-image': 'pin',
        'icon-size': 0.8,
        'icon-allow-overlap': true
      }
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Copy-paste this into a blank HTML file, open it in a browser, and you’ll see a
pin marker on the map. If it works, the problem in your app is simply the
old callback-style `loadImage`.

---

## 6️⃣ Next steps for your project

1. **Replace every `map.loadImage(..., callback)`** with the promise version.  
   A quick global search (`loadImage(`) will usually find all occurrences.

2. **Check your style JSON** (or the URL you’re using) for any `sprite`,
   `glyphs`, or `source` URLs that still start with `mapbox://`. Replace them
   with open-source equivalents or host your own assets.

3. **Run the app in the browser console** and look for warnings like  
   `Failed to load sprite …` or `Failed to load glyphs …`. Those indicate
   additional Mapbox-specific URLs that need swapping.

4. **If you use other Mapbox-only APIs** (e.g. `setFog`, `getFreeCameraOptions`,
   `setRTLTextPlugin`), follow the **Step 4** section of the migration guide
   (the skill you just read) to replace them with the MapLibre equivalents.

5. **Test on a clean build** (clear caches, hard-reload) to make sure the
   new images are being fetched and not served from a stale Service Worker.

---

### TL;DR

* `map.loadImage` now returns a promise → use `await`/`.then` and grab the
  `data` property.  
* Add the image **before** any layer that references it.  
* Ensure your style’s `sprite` (and `glyphs`) URLs are not `mapbox://`.  
* After those two changes your custom marker icons will appear again.

If you run into any other Mapbox-only calls (e.g. `setFog`, `setFreeCameraOptions`,
or plugins that still import `mapbox-gl`), let me know the exact code and I’ll
show you the MapLibre replacement. Happy mapping!

