answer:
### What’s happening?

`map.loadImage` changed its **signature** when MapLibre moved from the Mapbox-GL-v1-compatible branch to the modern v4+ (and later v6) releases:

| Library version | `loadImage` signature |
|-----------------|-----------------------|
| **Mapbox GL JS** (all v1-v3) | `loadImage(url, callback)` – the callback is **required** |
| **MapLibre GL JS** v1.x (the “1-branch” that mimics Mapbox v1) | Same as Mapbox – you can still pass a callback |
| **MapLibre GL JS** v4.0.0 + (the current stable line) | Returns a **Promise** – the callback you pass is **ignored** |

Because you installed the **latest** `maplibre-gl` (v6 at the time of writing), the callback you supplied is never called, so `map.addImage` never runs and the custom marker icons never get added to the style. That’s why the console warns about the missing callback and why the icons disappear.

---

## Fix 1 – Use the Promise API

Replace the callback-style call with the promise-style call (or `async/await`). The resolved value is an object whose `data` property is an `ImageBitmap` (or an `HTMLImageElement` in older browsers).

```js
// -------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣  Wait until the map’s style is ready
// -------------------------------------------------
map.on('load', async () => {
  try {
    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 2️⃣  Load the image – returns a Promise
    // -------------------------------------------------
    const { data: img } = await map.loadImage('https://example.com/pin.png');

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 3️⃣  Register the image with a name you’ll use later
    // -------------------------------------------------
    // `true` as the second argument makes the image SDF (optional)
    map.addImage('pin', img, { sdf: false });

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 4️⃣  Now you can use the image in a layer or a marker
    // -------------------------------------------------
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'my-pins',
      type: 'symbol',
      source: {
        type: 'geojson',
        data: myGeojson
      },
      layout: {
        'icon-image': 'pin',
        'icon-size': 0.8
      }
    });
  } catch (err) {
    console.error('Failed to load marker image:', err);
  }
});
```

**If you prefer `.then()` syntax:**

```js
map.on('load', () => {
  map.loadImage('https://example.com/pin.png')
    .then(({ data }) => map.addImage('pin', data))
    .catch(err => console.error(err));
});
```

### Why the `map.on('load')` wrapper?

`loadImage` works **anytime** after the `Map` instance has been created, but you usually want to add the image **before** you add a layer that references it. The safest spot is inside the `load` event (or `styledata`/`sourcedata` if you’re swapping styles later).

---

## Fix 2 – If you really need the old callback API

If you cannot upgrade to the promise style right away (e.g., you’re stuck on a codebase that still uses callbacks everywhere), you have two options:

1. **Pin the 1-branch** of MapLibre, which still accepts callbacks:

   ```bash
   npm install maplibre-gl@1
   ```

   *Caveat*: The 1-branch is frozen at the Mapbox GL v1.13 API surface. It will not receive new features or bug-fixes, and it will eventually be deprecated. Use only as a temporary stop-gap.

2. **Wrap the promise yourself** to keep the old shape:

   ```js
   function loadImageCallback(url, cb) {
     map.loadImage(url)
       .then(({ data }) => cb(null, data))
       .catch(err => cb(err));
   }

   // usage – identical to the old Mapbox code
   loadImageCallback('https://example.com/pin.png', (err, img) => {
     if (err) throw err;
     map.addImage('pin', img);
   });
   ```

   This lets you keep the rest of your code unchanged while you gradually migrate.

---

## Fix 3 – Verify the sprite / glyph URLs (optional)

If you also see **missing default icons** (e.g., the built-in `marker-icon` from the Mapbox style), double-check that the style you’re using supplies a **`sprite`** and **`glyphs`** URL that are reachable without a Mapbox token. When you switched from a `mapbox://` style to an open-source style (e.g., OpenFreeMap, MapTiler, your own style JSON), you may have left a stray `sprite` or `glyphs` reference that still points to `mapbox://sprites/...` or `mapbox://fonts/...`. Those URLs will 401 and cause the whole style to fall back to a blank map or missing icons.

*Quick test*: Open the style URL in a browser and look for `"sprite"` and `"glyphs"` fields. If they start with `mapbox://`, replace them with a public URL (or remove them if you don’t need them). For a quick, token-free style you can use the OpenFreeMap example from the migration guide:

```js
style: 'https://tiles.openfreemap.org/styles/liberty'
```

That style already bundles a working sprite and glyph set.

---

## TL;DR Checklist for Your Situation

| ✅ | Action |
|---|--------|
| 1 | **Replace the callback** with the promise version of `map.loadImage`. |
| 2 | Ensure the call runs **after the map has loaded** (`map.on('load', …)`). |
| 3 | Verify that the **style’s `sprite` and `glyphs` URLs** are not `mapbox://` (otherwise other icons will stay missing). |
| 4 | If you need a quick fix and cannot change the code now, either **pin `maplibre-gl@1`** or **wrap the promise** in a small helper that mimics the old callback signature. |
| 5 | After the image is added, reference it in a layer (`icon-image: 'pin'`) or in a `new maplibregl.Marker({ element: img })` if you’re using DOM-based markers. |

---

### Full Minimal Example (ES M modules)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – custom marker</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6.0.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    #map { width: 100%; height: 100vh; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script type="module">
  import * as maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6.0.0/dist/maplibre-gl.mjs';

  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://tiles.openfreemap.org/styles/liberty', // any non-Mapbox style
    center: [-122.42, 37.78],
    zoom: 12
  });

  map.on('load', async () => {
    try {
      const { data: img } = await map.loadImage(
        'https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mapbox/mapbox-gl-js/main/docs/assets/custom_marker.png'
      );
      map.addImage('my-pin', img);

      // Example layer that uses the image
      map.addLayer({
        id: 'pin-layer',
        type: 'symbol',
        source: {
          type: 'geojson',
          data: {
            type: 'FeatureCollection',
            features: [{
              type: 'Feature',
              geometry: { type: 'Point', coordinates: [-122.42, 37.78] }
            }]
          }
        },
        layout: {
          'icon-image': 'my-pin',
          'icon-size': 0.7
        }
      });
    } catch (e) {
      console.error('loadImage failed:', e);
    }
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Run that snippet (or drop it into your project) and you’ll see the custom pin appear—proving that the promise-based `loadImage` works with the latest MapLibre.

---

### Need more help?

* If you still get a 401/403 error, double-check that **no `mapbox://` URLs** remain in your style JSON (sources, sprite, glyphs).
* If you’re using **Mapbox-specific plugins** (e.g., `@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder`), replace them with the MapLibre equivalents (`@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder`, `@maplibre/maplibre-gl-compare`, etc.).
* For a deeper dive on the callback-to-promise migration, see the **“Call-backs became promises (GL JS v4.0.0)”** section of the migration guide.

Feel free to paste the exact snippet you’re using if you run into any other errors!

