    // 2️⃣  Load the image – returns a Promise
    // -------------------------------------------------
    const { data: img } = await map.loadImage('https://example.com/pin.png');

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 3️⃣  Register the image with a name you’ll use later
    // -------------------------------------------------
    // `true` as the second argument makes the image SDF (optional)
    map.addImage('pin', img, { sdf: false });

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 4️⃣  Now you can use the image in a layer or a marker
    // -------------------------------------------------
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'my-pins',
      type: 'symbol',
      source: {
        type: 'geojson',
        data: myGeojson
      },
      layout: {
        'icon-image': 'pin',
        'icon-size': 0.8
      }
    });
  } catch (err) {
    console.error('Failed to load marker image:', err);
  }
});
```

**If you prefer `.then()` syntax:**

```js
map.on('load', () => {
  map.loadImage('https://example.com/pin.png')
    .then(({ data }) => map.addImage('pin', data))
    .catch(err => console.error(err));
});
```

### Why the `map.on('load')` wrapper?

`loadImage` works **anytime** after the `Map` instance has been created, but you usually want to add the image **before** you add a layer that references it. The safest spot is inside the `load` event (or `styledata`/`sourcedata` if you’re swapping styles later).

---

## Fix 2 – If you really need the old callback API

If you cannot upgrade to the promise style right away (e.g., you’re stuck on a codebase that still uses callbacks everywhere), you have two options:

1. **Pin the 1-branch** of MapLibre, which still accepts callbacks:

   ```bash
   npm install maplibre-gl@1
   ```

   *Caveat*: The 1-branch is frozen at the Mapbox GL v1.13 API surface. It will not receive new features or bug-fixes, and it will eventually be deprecated. Use only as a temporary stop-gap.

2. **Wrap the promise yourself** to keep the old shape:

   ```js
   function loadImageCallback(url, cb) {
     map.loadImage(url)
       .then(({ data }) => cb(null, data))
       .catch(err => cb(err));
   }

   // usage – identical to the old Mapbox code
   loadImageCallback('https://example.com/pin.png', (err, img) => {
     if (err) throw err;
     map.addImage('pin', img);
   });
   ```

   This lets you keep the rest of your code unchanged while you gradually migrate.

---

## Fix 3 – Verify the sprite / glyph URLs (optional)

If you also see **missing default icons** (e.g., the built-in `marker-icon` from the Mapbox style), double-check that the style you’re using supplies a **`sprite`** and **`glyphs`** URL that are reachable without a Mapbox token. When you switched from a `mapbox://` style to an open-source style (e.g., OpenFreeMap, MapTiler, your own style JSON), you may have left a stray `sprite` or `glyphs` reference that still points to `mapbox://sprites/...` or `mapbox://fonts/...`. Those URLs will 401 and cause the whole style to fall back to a blank map or missing icons.

*Quick test*: Open the style URL in a browser and look for `"sprite"` and `"glyphs"` fields. If they start with `mapbox://`, replace them with a public URL (or remove them if you don’t need them). For a quick, token-free style you can use the OpenFreeMap example from the migration guide:

```js
style: 'https://tiles.openfreemap.org/styles/liberty'
```

That style already bundles a working sprite and glyph set.

---

## TL;DR Checklist for Your Situation

| ✅ | Action |
|---|--------|
| 1 | **Replace the callback** with the promise version of `map.loadImage`. |
| 2 | Ensure the call runs **after the map has loaded** (`map.on('load', …)`). |
| 3 | Verify that the **style’s `sprite` and `glyphs` URLs** are not `mapbox://` (otherwise other icons will stay missing). |
| 4 | If you need a quick fix and cannot change the code now, either **pin `maplibre-gl@1`** or **wrap the promise** in a small helper that mimics the old callback signature. |
| 5 | After the image is added, reference it in a layer (`icon-image: 'pin'`) or in a `new maplibregl.Marker({ element: img })` if you’re using DOM-based markers. |

---

### Full Minimal Example (ES M modules)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – custom marker</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6.0.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    #map { width: 100%; height: 100vh; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script type="module">
  import * as maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6.0.0/dist/maplibre-gl.mjs';

  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://tiles.openfreemap.org/styles/liberty', // any non-Mapbox style
    center: [-122.42, 37.78],
    zoom: 12
  });

  map.on('load', async () => {
    try {
      const { data: img } = await map.loadImage(
        'https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mapbox/mapbox-gl-js/main/docs/assets/custom_marker.png'
      );
      map.addImage('my-pin', img);

      // Example layer that uses the image
      map.addLayer({
        id: 'pin-layer',
        type: 'symbol',
        source: {
          type: 'geojson',
          data: {
            type: 'FeatureCollection',
            features: [{
              type: 'Feature',
              geometry: { type: 'Point', coordinates: [-122.42, 37.78] }
            }]
          }
        },
        layout: {
          'icon-image': 'my-pin',
          'icon-size': 0.7
        }
      });
    } catch (e) {
      console.error('loadImage failed:', e);
    }
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Run that snippet (or drop it into your project) and you’ll see the custom pin appear—proving that the promise-based `loadImage` works with the latest MapLibre.

---

### Need more help?

* If you still get a 401/403 error, double-check that **no `mapbox://` URLs** remain in your style JSON (sources, sprite, glyphs).
* If you’re using **Mapbox-specific plugins** (e.g., `@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder`), replace them with the MapLibre equivalents (`@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder`, `@maplibre/maplibre-gl-compare`, etc.).
* For a deeper dive on the callback-to-promise migration, see the **“Call-backs became promises (GL JS v4.0.0)”** section of the migration guide.

Feel free to paste the exact snippet you’re using if you run into any other errors!

answer:
### What’s happening?

1. **`map.loadImage` changed in MapLibre v4.0.0** – it no longer accepts a callback.  
   It now **returns a `Promise`** that resolves with an object `{ data: ImageBitmap }`.  
   Because you’re still passing a callback, the promise is created and then silently ignored, so the image never gets added to the style.  

2. **Your marker icons disappear** because the custom image (`'pin'`) is never added to the style’s image registry.  
   When the style tries to draw a symbol layer that references `icon-image: 'pin'`, the lookup fails and the symbol is rendered as a transparent “missing-image” placeholder (which looks like the icon is gone).

---

## Fix #1 – Use the promise-based API

```js
// ✅ Modern MapLibre (v4+)
async function addPinImage(map, url) {
  try {
    // loadImage resolves to { data: ImageBitmap }
    const { data: img } = await map.loadImage(url);
    // addImage can be called any time after the map has been created,
    // but it must be *after* the style is loaded (or you’ll get
    // “Style is not loaded” errors).  The safest place is inside
    // map.on('style.load') or map.on('load').
    map.addImage('pin', img);
  } catch (err) {
    console.error('Failed to load pin image:', err);
    // you can decide whether to fall back to a default icon here
  }
}

// Example usage
map.on('load', () => {
  addPinImage(map, 'https://example.com/assets/pin.png');
});
```

If you prefer the classic `.then()` style:

```js
map.on('load', () => {
  map.loadImage('https://example.com/assets/pin.png')
    .then(({ data }) => map.addImage('pin', data))
    .catch(err => console.error('loadImage error', err));
});
```

### Why the `map.on('load')` guard?

* The style (including the sprite sheet) must be fully parsed before you can add new images.  
* Adding the image **before** the style is ready throws `Error: Style is not loaded` (you’ll see that in the console if you try).

---

## Fix #2 – Verify the image URL & CORS

* **CORS** – The image must be served with `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or your domain).  
  If the server blocks cross-origin requests, `loadImage` will reject with a CORS error and the icon will stay missing.  
* **HTTPS** – If your page is loaded over HTTPS, the image URL also needs to be HTTPS; mixed-content requests are blocked.

You can test the URL directly in the browser’s network tab. If you see a 200 response with the correct `Content-Type: image/png` and the `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` header, you’re good.

---

## Fix #3 – Double-check the style layer that uses the icon

```js
map.addLayer({
  id: 'my-pins',
  type: 'symbol',
  source: 'my-points',   // a GeoJSON source you added earlier
  layout: {
    'icon-image': 'pin',   // <-- must match the name you passed to addImage
    'icon-size': 0.8,
    'icon-allow-overlap': true
  }
});
```

If the layer’s `icon-image` property is misspelled or points to a different name, the icon will also be missing. After you successfully call `addImage('pin', …)`, the layer will instantly start rendering the icons (no need to reload the map).

---

## Full minimal example (MapLibre v6)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – custom marker</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6.0.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script type="module">
  import * as maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6.0.0/dist/maplibre-gl.mjs';

  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://tiles.openfreemap.org/styles/liberty', // any non-Mapbox style
    center: [-122.42, 37.78],
    zoom: 12
  });

  // Add a GeoJSON source with a few points
  map.on('load', () => {
    map.addSource('my-points', {
      type: 'geojson',
      data: {
        type: 'FeatureCollection',
        features: [
          { type: 'Feature', geometry: { type: 'Point', coordinates: [-122.42, 37.78] } },
          { type: 'Feature', geometry: { type: 'Point', coordinates: [-122.45, 37.80] } }
        ]
      }
    });

    // Load the custom pin image (promise-based)
    map.loadImage('https://raw.githubusercontent.com/maplibre/maplibre-gl-js/main/docs/assets/pin.png')
      .then(({ data }) => {
        map.addImage('pin', data);
        // Now add the layer that uses the image
        map.addLayer({
          id: 'my-pins',
          type: 'symbol',
          source: 'my-points',
          layout: {
            'icon-image': 'pin',
            'icon-size': 0.7,
            'icon-allow-overlap': true
          }
        });
      })
      .catch(err => console.error('❌ loadImage failed', err));
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Run the snippet (or copy it into a local file) and you’ll see the two pins appear. The key points are:

* **`loadImage` → promise**  
* **`addImage` after the style is loaded**  
* **CORS-compatible image URL**  

---

## Quick checklist for “marker icons are gone”

| ✅ Done? | Item |
|--------|------|
| ☐ | Replace `map.loadImage(url, cb)` with the promise version (`await` or `.then`). |
| ☐ | Call the image-loading code **inside** `map.on('load')` (or `style.load`). |
| ☐ | Verify the image URL is reachable, served over HTTPS, and has `Access-Control-Allow-Origin:*`. |
| ☐ | Ensure the name you pass to `addImage` (`'pin'` in the example) exactly matches the `icon-image` value in the symbol layer. |
| ☐ | If you’re using a custom style, make sure the style’s `sprite` and `glyphs` URLs are also non-Mapbox (e.g., OpenFreeMap or your own host). |
| ☐ | Open the browser console → “Network” tab → confirm the image request returns **200** and not a 401/403. |

---

## TL;DR code snippet

```js
map.on('load', async () => {
  try {
    const { data: img } = await map.loadImage('https://my.cdn/pin.png');
    map.addImage('pin', img);               // registers the image
    map.addLayer({                          // uses it
      id: 'pins',
      type: 'symbol',
      source: 'my-points',
      layout: { 'icon-image': 'pin', 'icon-size': 0.8 }
    });
  } catch (e) {
    console.error('Could not load pin image', e);
  }
});
```

Once you switch to the promise-based API and load the image after the style is ready, the icons will appear again. 🎉

